.TH MESHVIEW 2
.SH NAME
meshview: new, cmd, draw \- declarative field-to-pixel colour ramps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "mesh.m";
include "meshview.m";
meshview := load Meshview Meshview->PATH;
View: import meshview;

v := meshview->new(image, font);
v.cmd("colour 0 1 16r000030ff 16r0080e0ff 16re0ff40ff 16rffff00ff");
v.draw(image.r, grid, values);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Meshview
turns a
.IR mesh (2)
grid's worth of scalar values into pixels \(em the same relationship
.IR plot (2)
has to a time-series chart, applied to a 2-D field instead: describe
the colour ramp once via
.BR cmd ,
then
.B draw
every frame, rather than hand-building a palette table and per-cell
blit loop for each field.
.PP
.B New
takes the destination image and a font (colours are resolved via the
font's display, following
.IR plot (2)'s
own convention \(em
.B image
may still be
.B nil
at setup time, before the first reshape/onscreen, while a font opened
against the window's display already isn't).
.PP
.B Cmd
accepts:
.TP
.BI "colour " lo " " hi " " rgba0 " " rgba1 " [" rgba2 " ...]"
Maps
.RI [ lo , hi ]
onto a 256-entry ramp, linearly interpolated between the given
.B 16rRRGGBBAA
stops, evenly spaced across the range.  Two stops give a simple
gradient; three or more give a fuller heat ramp.
.PP
.B Draw
renders
.IR grid 's
.I nx
by
.I ny
.I values
into
.IR r ,
one sub-rectangle per cell.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/meshview.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/meshview.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mesh (2),
.IR plot (2),
.IR pdelab (1)
